### Introduction

`backend/css.prog` isn't loaded by default -- it needs an explicit `@load`. CSS is plain data here: a handful of structs (`Property`, `Style_Rule`, `At_Rule`, ...) build a small AST by hand, and a single `Css_Formatter_Visitor` walks whatever tree you built and turns it into a real CSS string. No parser -- you construct the tree directly.
###
@load 'frontend/css.prog'

rule := Style_Rule(['.box'], [Property('color', 'red'), Property('padding', '8px')])
css   := Css_Formatter_Visitor().format(rule)

@assert css == ".box {\n    color: red;\n    padding: 8px;\n}", 'format should pretty-print a rule with one declaration per line'


### minify

The same visitor, constructed with `minify := true`, collapses everything onto one line with no extra whitespace -- same tree, same method, different output:
###
minified := Css_Formatter_Visitor(minify := true).format(rule)
@assert minified == '.box{color:red;padding:8px;}', 'minify should collapse the same rule onto one line'


### Nested rules get a synthesized &

A `Style_Rule` can nest more rules inside its own `rules` -- real CSS nesting. A nested selector that doesn't already start with `&`/`:` gets one synthesized in front of it:
###
nested := Style_Rule(['.box'], [], [Style_Rule(['.inner'], [Property('color', 'blue')])])
@assert Css_Formatter_Visitor().format(nested) == ".box {\n    & .inner {\n        color: blue;\n    }\n}", 'a rule nested inside another rule should get a synthesized & prefix'


### At-rules and @scope don't synthesize &

A rule merely sitting inside an `At_Rule`'s (`@media`, ...) or `Scope_Rule`'s (`@scope`) own body isn't "nested" the same way -- there's no parent selector for `&` to refer to, so nothing gets added:
###
media := At_Rule('media', '(min-width: 100px)', [Style_Rule(['.a'], [Property('color', 'red')])])
@assert Css_Formatter_Visitor().format(media) == "@media (min-width: 100px) {\n    .a {\n        color: red;\n    }\n}", 'a rule inside an At_Rule body should not get a synthesized &'

scope := Scope_Rule('.widget', '', [Style_Rule(['p'], [Property('margin', '0')])])
@assert Css_Formatter_Visitor().format(scope) == "@scope (.widget) {\n    p {\n        margin: 0;\n    }\n}", 'a rule inside a Scope_Rule body should not get a synthesized & either'


### A Stylesheet is just a list of top-level rules

`format` dispatches on whatever struct kind it's handed -- a `Stylesheet` formats each of its rules and joins them, with a blank line between in pretty mode:
###
sheet := Stylesheet([Style_Rule(['.a'], [Property('color', 'red')]), Style_Rule(['.b'], [Property('color', 'blue')])])
@assert Css_Formatter_Visitor().format(sheet) == ".a {\n    color: red;\n}\n\n.b {\n    color: blue;\n}", 'a Stylesheet should format each rule and join them with a blank line'
@assert Css_Formatter_Visitor(minify := true).format(sheet) == '.a{color:red;}.b{color:blue;}', 'minify should join rules with no separator at all'


### Css_Lint_Visitor

A separate visitor walks the same kind of tree looking for problems instead of formatting it: duplicate properties, hardcoded vendor prefixes, and redundant zero-units (`0px` where `0` would do). `.lint` resets its own warnings list at the start of every call, so one instance can be reused across trees:
###
flagged := Style_Rule(['.box'], [
	Property('color', 'red'),
	Property('color', 'blue'),
	Property('-webkit-transform', 'none'),
	Property('margin', '0px 10px')
])

warnings := Css_Lint_Visitor().lint(flagged)
@assert warnings == [
	"Duplicate property 'color'",
	"Avoid hardcoded vendor prefix in '-webkit-transform'",
	"Unit unneeded for zero value in 'margin: 0px 10px'"
], 'lint should flag all three problems, in the order the declarations appear'

clean := Style_Rule(['.box'], [Property('color', 'red'), Property('padding', '10px')])
@assert Css_Lint_Visitor().lint(clean) == [], 'a rule with no problems should produce no warnings'


### Custom indent size

Both visitors take an `indent_size` as their first constructor argument (default 4) -- everything else about pretty-printing stays the same, just narrower or wider:
###
narrow := Css_Formatter_Visitor(2).format(Style_Rule(['.box'], [Property('color', 'red')]))
@assert narrow == ".box {\n  color: red;\n}", 'a custom indent_size should be honored throughout'
